WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.600
Think about a family budget. If a family earns more than it spends, it has money left to save. If it spends more than it earns, it must borrow. Governments are the same.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:25.491
Government revenue is the money the government receives, mostly from taxes. Government expenditure is what it spends, for example on health, schools and benefits.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:37.705
A fiscal surplus is when government revenue is greater than government expenditure in a year. A fiscal deficit is when expenditure is greater than revenue, so the government must borrow the difference.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:50.941
For example, revenue forty billion dollars and spending forty-five billion. Forty minus forty-five is minus five billion. That is a deficit, so the government borrows five billion dollars.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:01:13.263
Step one, define the term by comparing revenue and expenditure. Step two, choose a consequence. A surplus can repay debt, cut taxes, or pay for services. A deficit means borrowing and interest, maybe higher taxes later, but a short-run boost to demand.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:25.474
Step three, chain it through to who is affected. Repaying debt means less interest each year, so more money is left for services that households use.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:39.897
Step four, use the figures given. Step five, in an Assess answer, weigh the other side. A surplus may mean taxes are too high, or services were cut. Then judge.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:02:01.157
Here is a real question from June 2022. Nauru, a tiny island with ten thousand, eight hundred and twenty-four people, had a fiscal surplus of sixteen point one per cent of G D P in 2019. Analyse the benefits of a fiscal surplus for Nauru. Six marks.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:14.261
Define it: revenue, mostly from taxes, is greater than spending over a year. A surplus of sixteen point one per cent of G D P is very large, so the government has a lot of spare money.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:26.373
Benefit one. The government can repay past borrowing. It then pays less interest each year, so more revenue can go on services, and its finances are safer.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:41.331
Benefit two. The surplus could go back to the ten thousand, eight hundred and twenty-four residents as lower taxes. Their disposable income rises, so they spend more, and local firms see more demand.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:58.132
Benefit three. Spend it on healthcare or education. Healthier, better-trained workers are more productive, raising output and growth in the long run. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:12.360
A common mistake is mixing up the fiscal balance with the current account. Exports and imports belong to the current account. The fiscal position compares government revenue and spending.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:22.761
Another is saying a surplus helps the economy without saying how it is used. Name the use, then the effect.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:40.749
Now you try. An example country collects thirty billion dollars in revenue and spends thirty-six billion. Is this a fiscal surplus or a deficit? Then explain one consequence. Pause and decide.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:53.121
Revenue minus expenditure: thirty minus thirty-six is minus six billion dollars. Spending is bigger, so it is a fiscal deficit of six billion dollars.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:04:04.019
The government must borrow six billion dollars. National debt rises and interest must be paid, which may mean higher taxes or spending cuts later.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:16.310
Remember: define by comparing revenue and expenditure, choose a consequence, chain it to the people affected, and use the data. Now try the practice questions.
